{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-277891",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{3}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $12,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{12}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{3}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{12}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{12}={x}^{3+12}={x}^{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $15.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
